   ['Muscle fibre', 'Myofibril', 'Sarcomere', 'Actin', 'Myosin', 'Neuromuscular junction', 'Acetylcholine', 'Cross-bridge'],
   ['A skeletal muscle is made of bundles of long, multinucleated cells (muscle fibres), each packed with myofibrils. Myofibrils consist of repeating units called sarcomeres, from one Z line to the next, containing thin actin filaments and thick myosin filaments, which give the striped (striated) appearance.',
    'Contraction begins when a motor neuron releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. This triggers an action potential in the muscle fibre, which spreads along T-tubules and causes the sarcoplasmic reticulum to release calcium ions. Calcium binds to troponin, moving tropomyosin away from binding sites on actin. Myosin heads attach (cross-bridges), pivot (power stroke) to pull actin towards the centre of the sarcomere, detach when ATP binds, and re-cock as ATP is split. The sarcomere shortens while the filaments themselves stay the same length: this is the sliding filament theory (Huxley and Hanson, 1954).',
    'Relaxation occurs when calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. ATP is needed both for the power stroke and for detachment; after death, ATP runs out and cross-bridges stay attached, causing rigor mortis. Muscles work in antagonistic pairs: when the biceps contracts to flex the elbow, the triceps relaxes; the triceps contracts to extend it.'],
